# Layered architecture

Every feature uses this call chain:

`Controller` → `XxxService` (interface) → `XxxServiceImpl` → `XxxDao` (interface) → `XxxDaoImpl` → `XxxRepository`

## Controller
- HTTP only: map the request, read the authenticated user, validate input, call one service method, build the response.
- No business rules, calculations, domain decisions, or data access.
- Inject the service interface (`PetService`). Never inject `*ServiceImpl`, a DAO, or a repository.

## Service interface
- `XxxService` is an interface. It declares operations and contains no logic.
- Controllers and other callers depend on `XxxService`, never on `XxxServiceImpl`.

## Service implementation
- `XxxServiceImpl implements XxxService` and owns all business logic.
- `XxxServiceImpl` depends on DAO interfaces only (`PetDao`, `UserDao`).
- A class in a `service` package must never import, inject, or call a class in a `repository` package.

## DAO
- `XxxDao` is an interface between the service and the repository.
- `XxxDaoImpl implements XxxDao` (`@Repository`) and is the only layer allowed to call a Spring Data repository.
- DAO methods load and persist data. Business rules stay in `XxxServiceImpl`.

## Forbidden
- Domain `if`/`else`, calculations, or persistence calls inside a controller.
- `XxxServiceImpl` (or any service class) calling `XxxRepository` and skipping the DAO.
- Business rules inside `XxxDaoImpl`.

## Reference
- `PetController` injects `PetService`
- `PetServiceImpl implements PetService` and calls `PetDao`
- `PetDaoImpl implements PetDao` and calls `PetsRepository`
